function updateUserPassword($userId, $newPassword) {
    static $rateLimiter = []; // In-memory rate limiter

    // Rate limit concurrent requests
    if (isset($rateLimiter[$userId])) {
        sleep(1); // Sleep for 1 second before retrying to respect the rate limit
        return;
    }
    $rateLimiter[$userId] = time() + 60; // Set a new 60-second window for next request

    // Validate password complexity
    if (!password_validator($newPassword)) {
        unset($rateLimiter[$userId]); // Remove from rate limiter on validation failure
        return 'Invalid password format';
    }

    try {
        // Attempt to update the password in the database
        $sql = "UPDATE users SET password=?, failed_attempts+=1 WHERE id=?";
        $stmt = dbConnect()->prepare($sql);

        // Hash the new password before storing it in the database
        $hashedPassword = password_hash($newPassword, PASSWORD_DEFAULT);
        $stmt->execute([$hashedPassword, $userId]);

        // Reset failed attempts counter if successful
        $sql = "UPDATE users SET failed_attempts=0 WHERE id=?";
        $stmt = dbConnect()->prepare($sql);
        $stmt->execute([$userId]);
    } catch (Exception $e) {
        // Retry on transient database errors, but limit retries to 3 times
        static $retryCount = 0;
        if ($e->getCode() === dbConnect()->errorCode() && $retryCount < 3) {
            sleep(rand(100, 500) / 1000); // Random delay to reduce load on database
            return updateUserPassword($userId, $newPassword);
        }

        // Log password update attempts and failure reasons
        logPasswordUpdateAttempt($userId, $e->getMessage());

        unset($rateLimiter[$userId]); // Remove from rate limiter on failure
        return 'Failed to update password. Please try again later.';
    }

    unset($rateLimiter[$userId]); // Remove from rate limiter on success
    return 'Password updated successfully.';
}

function password_validator($password) {
    // Predefined complexity rules for the new password (modify as needed)
    $minLength = 8;
    $specialChars = ['!', '@', '#', '$', '%', '^', '&', '*', '(', ')', '_', '+', '-', '{', '}', '|', '~', '<', '>', ',', '.'];

    if (strlen($password) < $minLength) {
        return false;
    }

    if (!preg_match('`[a-z]`', $password)) {
        return false;
    }

    if (!preg_match('`[A-Z]`', $password)) {
        return false;
}

if (!in_array(strtolower($password[0]), $specialChars)) {
    return false;
}

return true;
}
```

This PHP function, `updateUserPassword`, handles the task of updating a user's password within a high-traffic web application. It validates the new password against predefined complexity rules, manages concurrency using an in-memory rate limiter, and gracefully handles errors by retrying transient failures up to 3 times before logging the failure and returning an error message to the user.

The function optimizes database queries by using prepared statements and minimizing load with a random delay for transient errors. It also logs all password update attempts for auditing purposes, while considering trade-offs between memory usage, response time, and code complexity by using static variables to store retry counts and rate limiting data in memory.']

['```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct State {
    int id;
    struct State* next;
} State;

typedef struct FSA {
    State* states;
    int num_states;
    pthread_mutex_t mutex;
    FILE* log_file;
    enum LogLevel { SILENT, BASIC, DETAILED } log_level;
} FSA;

FSA* create_fsa(int num_states) {
    FSA* fsa = malloc(sizeof(FSA));
    if (!fsa) return NULL;

    fsa->num_states = num_states;
    fsa->states = calloc(num_states, sizeof(State));
    if (!fsa->states) {
        free(fsa);
        return NULL;
    }

    pthread_mutex_init(&fsa->mutex, NULL);
    fsa->log_file = stderr;
    fsa->log_level = SILENT;

    return fsa;
}

void log(FSA* fsa, const char* message) {
    if (fsa->log_level > SILENT) {
        time_t timestamp = time(NULL);
        struct tm* localtime = localtime(&timestamp);
        fprintf(fsa->log_file, "[%d-%02d-%02d %02d:%02d:%02d][Thread ID: %lu] %s\n",
                1900 + localtime->tm_year, 1 + localtime->tm_mon, localtime->tm_mday,
                localtime->tm_hour, localtime->tm_min, localtime->tm_sec, pthread_self(), message);
    }
}

State* get_state(FSA* fsa, int state_id) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&fsa->mutex);
    State* state = &fsa->states[state_id];
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&fsa->mutex);
    log(fsa, "Getting state");
    return state;
}

void update_state(FSA* fsa, State* state, int next_state) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&fsa->mutex);
    state->next = &fsa->states[next_state];
    log(fsa, "Updating state");
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&fsa->mutex);
}

void set_log_level(FSA* fsa, enum LogLevel level) {
    fsa->log_level = level;
}

State* get_linked_list_of_states(FSA* fsa, State* start_state, int last_state) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&fsa->mutex);
    State *current_state = start_state;
    State *head = NULL, *tail = NULL;

    while (current_state != &fsa->states[last_state]) {
        tail = current_state;
        current_state = current_state->next;
    }

    if (!head) {
        head = tail = current_state;
    } else {
        tail->next = current_state;
        tail = current_state;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&fsa->mutex);
    log(fsa, "Creating linked list of states");
    return head;
}
```
This code provides a basic implementation for the given task. It creates an FSA struct with thread-safe concurrent access using `pthread_mutex_t`, logs state transitions and changes made to the FSA, and dynamically allocates and returns a linked list of states from a given finite state automaton (FSA). The logging mechanism is configurable to support different levels of detail and storage options. Optimizations for resource-constrained environments are not explicit in this implementation but consider using efficient data structures like singly-linked lists, minimizing memory allocations, and utilizing appropriate logging configurations based on system requirements.']

