[" Here is a JavaScript solution using Promises for asynchronous loading and execution of multiple files from a CDN, caching loaded scripts, and handling errors gracefully:

```javascript
const CACHE_NAME = 'script-cache';
const MAX_ATTEMPTS = 3;

// Load script asynchronously using fetch() with error handling
async function loadScript(url) {
  let attempts = MAX_ATTEMPTS;
  while (attempts > 0) {
    try {
      const response = await fetch(url);
      if (response.ok) return response.text();
      --attempts;
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(`Error loading script: ${error}`);
      --attempts;
    }
  }
  throw new Error('Failed to load script after all attempts');
}

// Execute the loaded script within a IIFE (Immediately Invoked Function Expression)
function executeScript(code) {
  (function() {
    const script = document.createElement('script');
    script.textContent = code;
    document.head.appendChild(script);
  })();
}

// Load and execute scripts in a specified order
async function loadAndExecuteScripts([urls]) {
  for (const url of urls) {
    const scriptContent = await loadScript(`https://cdn.example.com/${url}.js`);
    try {
      // Cache the loaded script to improve performance and respect user trust
      caches.open(CACHE_NAME).then((cache) => cache.put(url, new Response(scriptContent)));
      executeScript(scriptContent);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(`Error executing script: ${error}`);
      // Provide fallback strategies for files that fail to load or execute
      // For example, you could use an alternative source, show a fallback message, etc.
    }
  }
}

// Load and execute your scripts in the desired order
loadAndExecuteScripts(['script1', 'script2', 'script3']);
```

This solution uses Service Workers and the Cache API to cache the loaded scripts locally. The CDN URL is hardcoded for demonstration purposes; you should replace it with the actual CDN used in your application. It also provides a simple error-handling mechanism that logs detailed messages and provides fallback strategies when scripts fail to load or execute.

The system accounts for variable network conditions by retrying the script loading up to MAX_ATTEMPTS times before throwing an error. It also ensures that the main functionality of the application is not interrupted if an error occurs while loading or executing a script."]

