To handle concurrent requests efficiently, this solution uses a pool to minimize dynamic memory allocation calls, which can be costly in terms of performance. When there are not enough structures available in the pool, it dynamically allocates additional ones, handling errors gracefully if there is insufficient memory.

The function is optimized for performance by using a pool and merging contiguous blocks of unused structures to reduce the number of dynamic allocation calls and minimize fragmentation. At the same time, it remains robust under heavy load conditions due to the pool's ability to handle concurrent requests efficiently.

