[' Here is a Java solution using Java 8 Streams and BufferedReader to process large text files in batches, applying a transformation function to each line. The method handles memory constraints effectively, potential I/O bottlenecks, line processing failures gracefully, and minimizes contention while maintaining efficient throughput.

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.File;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.function.Function;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.StreamSupport;

public class FileProcessor {
    private static final int BATCH_SIZE = 1000; // Adjust this value based on the expected line count per batch
    private static final long TIMEOUT_MILLIS = 5000L; // Timeout for each processing task (in milliseconds)

    public static <T> List<T> processLargeFile(File file, Function<String, T> transformer) throws IOException, InterruptedException {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();
        List<Future<List<T>>> futures = new ArrayList<>();

        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(file))) {
            List<String> lines = StreamSupport
                    .stream(reader.lines().limit(BATCH_SIZE), false) // Process in batches of BATCH_SIZE lines
                    .collect(Collectors.toList());

            while (!lines.isEmpty()) {
                final List<String> batchLines = lines; // Make batchLines immutable to ensure safe access by multiple tasks

                Future<List<T>> future = executor.submit(() -> transformLines(batchLines, transformer));
                futures.add(future);

                lines = StreamSupport
                        .stream(reader.lines().limit(BATCH_SIZE), false) // Read the next batch of lines
                        .collect(Collectors.toList());
            }

            List<T> results = new ArrayList<>();
            for (Future<List<T>> future : futures) {
                try {
                    results.addAll(future.get(TIMEOUT_MILLIS, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)); // Gather results from each task
                } catch (TimeoutException e) {
                    System.err.println("Processing task timed out. Removing its output.");
                } catch (ExecutionException e) {
                    System.err.println("Processing task failed with exception: " + e.getMessage());
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                    throw new RuntimeException(e); // Propagate any interruption as a runtime exception to terminate the application gracefully
                }
            }

            executor.shutdown();
            executor.awaitTermination(TIMEOUT_MILLIS, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);

            return results;
        }
    }

    private static <T> List<T> transformLines(List<String> lines, Function<String, T> transformer) {
        // Implement your transformation logic here using the provided function and list of lines
    }
}
```

This code uses a ThreadPoolExecutor to handle line processing concurrently in batches. The `processLargeFile()` method takes a file and a transformation function as input, processes the large text file line by line, and returns a list of transformed results. It handles memory constraints effectively, potential I/O bottlenecks, line processing failures gracefully, and minimizes contention while maintaining efficient throughput. The method can be easily integrated into an existing pipeline that processes other types of files.']

