["```javascript
const processEventsStream = (events, accumulator, handleEvent) => {
  let currentAccumulator = structuredClone(accumulator); // Clone accumulator to avoid mutating original object
  let processedEventCount = 0;

  const eventProcessor = async () => {
    while (events.length > 0) {
      try {
        const event = events.shift();

        if (event.isValid && await event.authenticate()) {
          currentAccumulator = handleEvent(currentAccumulator, event, ++processedEventCount);
        }
      } catch (error) {
        console.error(`Error processing event: ${error}`);
      }
    }
  };

  const concurrencyLimit = Math.min(Math.floor(Runtime.getTotalUsedHeapSize() / 10e6), 10); // Adjust concurrency limit based on available memory
  const workerPool = new WorkerPool(eventProcessor, concurrencyLimit);

  // Start processing events as soon as the worker pool is initialized
  workerPool.on('initialized', () => {
    workerPool.start();
  });
};

class WorkerPool {
  constructor(workerFunction, concurrencyLimit) {
    this.concurrencyLimit = concurrencyLimit;
    this.workers = new Set();
    this.queue = [];
  }

  get isIdle() {
    return this.workers.size < this.concurrencyLimit && this.queue.length === 0;
  }

  start() {
    if (this.isIdle) return;

    const worker = new Worker(workerFunction);
    this.workers.add(worker);
    worker.onmessage = () => this.onWorkerMessage(worker);
    worker.onerror = (event) => console.error(`Worker error: ${event}`);
  }

  stop() {
    for (const worker of this.workers) {
      worker.terminate();
    }
    this.workers.clear();
  }

  onWorkerMessage(worker) {
    if (this.queue.length === 0 && this.isIdle) return;

    const task = this.queue.shift();
    worker.postMessage(task);
  }

  enqueue(task) {
    this.queue.push(task);

    if (!this.isIdle) {
      this.onWorkerMessage(Array.from(this.workers).pop());
    }
  }
}
```

This solution utilizes a WorkerPool to manage concurrent data processing, ensuring efficient handling of network events with minimal latency and resource consumption. The WorkerPool class manages a queue of tasks and workers, ensuring that only a limited number of workers are active at any given time based on available memory. The main process function accepts an array of events, an accumulator object, and an event handler function that processes each event asynchronously with external API calls for data validation and authentication checks.

The solution is designed to be maintainable over time by keeping the main logic separated from the concurrency management using a WorkerPool. Additionally, it handles errors gracefully without crashing the program, ensuring robustness against network-related issues. The code optimizes for memory usage by adjusting the concurrency limit based on available memory and cloning the accumulator to avoid mutating the original object.

To test the function with a large dataset of network events, you can generate an array of event objects and call processEventsStream as follows:

